7592186045417] [17592186338962 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y-1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1df136f5-69d9-502e-8d2a-b817d7b3a8f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338962 #edb/ref 1074 "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-x-y-1 & =1 \\\\ z & =x+y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the plane $z=x+y+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338964 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{xy}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bc3409b-e78c-5e8f-bfcf-f26157077219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338964 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,2) & =\\frac{(2)-(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}{(1)(1)}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{xy} & =2 \\\\ z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ is the surface $z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380747 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186380748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380747 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380747 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380747 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "288a5fbe-4296-58f2-a71d-edcf3f852c57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380747 #edb/ref 1074 "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the paraboloid $z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186380750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380748 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "044bd578-4705-5aa8-80b4-fc8f6a1c787c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380749 #edb/ref 1080 "z=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380749 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380749 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4dd6f7b6-9fba-5e5a-b885-947e8862ee65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380750 #edb/ref 1080 "z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380750 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380750 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24dce5c8-935a-5b1f-b728-a2be7d268a35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380751 #edb/ref 