86409919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409921 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409921 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409921 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409921 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "595252cd-1afe-5f16-ae97-13c476966ff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409921 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\sqrt{5},3) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2(2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3)$ is the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a94aecad-6d06-5584-9ae6-ff5e4c972c6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfbdcc24-f373-559b-9261-b97d80ffba78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409925 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d981e48e-0b28-5d2c-b93e-70be17e1dbbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218